  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Aristotle and the Ontology of St. Bonaventure is already showing early scholarly uptake, with 2 citations in OpenAlex and two 2025 citing works that return to Bonaventure\u2019s metaphysics, Christology, and theology. That citation pattern, together with 2,098 recorded OAPEN downloads, suggests the book is being actively read as a resource for specialist research while remaining in an early stage of citation accumulation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Franziska van Buren\u2019s book contributes to medieval philosophy by reexamining Bonaventure through Aristotle rather than treating him simply as an anti-Aristotelian or narrowly Neo-platonic thinker. The subject matter is strongly aligned with ontology, interpretation, epistemology, and the history of philosophy, and the record shows that the work has already entered current scholarly conversations on Bonaventure\u2019s theology and metaphysics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The most concrete impact signal is citation uptake: OpenAlex records 2 citations, both in 2025. One citing work is a journal article in Religions on Bonaventure\u2019s Christology; the other is a dissertation on wisdom, metaphysics, and religion in Bonaventure\u2019s Collationes in Hexa\u00ebmeron. Taken together, these uses indicate that the book is being drawn into closely related research areas rather than only cited as background reading.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is stronger than the citation count alone might suggest. The book has 2,098 OAPEN downloads, which points to sustained reading and access through open and hybrid dissemination channels. OpenAIRE also records multiple repository instances, including open copies, and the book is listed as hybrid open access, supporting discoverability and reuse across institutional and library platforms.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record also indicates scholarly and educational positioning. Funding support is attributed to KU Leuven and the European Research Council, and the book aligns with the SDG theme of Quality Education. A WorldCat holding signal further suggests library visibility, while the limited but real citation trail indicates that the book is beginning to circulate within the community studying medieval philosophy and theology. Overall, the evidence points to a specialized monograph with clear early scholarly relevance and active readership, even though its citation base is still modest.</p>",
